{"id":"h095","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? Venus has no natural moons.","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"Venus has no known natural satellites.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"No confirmed moon of Venus has ever been detected by telescopes or spacecraft.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Venus and Mercury are the only two planets with no moons.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Historical reports of a Venusian moon such as Neith were never confirmed and are erroneous, likely optical artifacts or misidentified stars.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Spacecraft missions including Mariner, Venera, Pioneer Venus, and Magellan found no moon.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Venus has near-Earth asteroids such as 2002 VE68 that are quasi-satellites staying near Venus.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Quasi-satellites could be called minimoons or companions by some people.","confidence":0.6,"lean":-1,"theta":0.9425,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Quasi-satellites are not gravitationally bound to Venus and so are not natural moons in the standard sense.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Natural moons are normally defined as objects gravitationally orbiting a planet, and under this definition Venus has none.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"support